# TASK 1 – UNDERSTAND THE PROBLEM AND IDENTIFY THE OBJECTS

## 1. Problem Understanding

The Campus Smart-Parking System is designed to manage vehicle parking in a university campus. The university has multiple parking floors, and each floor contains a fixed number of parking slots.

The system allows students, staff and visitors to park their vehicles. It automatically allocates available parking slots, generates unique parking tickets, records entry time and calculates parking fees when vehicles leave.

## 2. Inputs

- Vehicle registration number
- Vehicle type
- Parking floor and slot
- Entry time and exit time
- User category
- Monthly pass details
- Electricity consumption for EVs

## 3. Outputs

- Allocated parking slot
- Unique parking ticket
- Parking duration
- Total parking fee
- EV charging fee
- Available parking slots

## 4. Candidate Classes

| Class | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Vehicle | Stores vehicle details |
| Car | Represents cars |
| TwoWheeler | Represents two-wheelers |
| ElectricCar | Represents electric cars |
| ParkingSlot | Manages individual parking slots |
| ParkingFloor | Manages slots on a floor |
| ParkingFacility | Manages the entire parking area |
| ParkingTicket | Stores ticket information |
| ParkingService | Handles parking operations |
| MonthlyPass | Validates monthly passes |

## 5. Business Rules

1. One slot can hold only one vehicle.
2. Every vehicle must have a unique registration number.
3. Every ticket must have a unique ticket number.
4. A vehicle can enter only if a suitable slot is available.
5. The slot must be released when the vehicle leaves.
6. Parking fees depend on vehicle type and duration.
7. Students and staff with valid monthly passes park free.
8. Visitors must pay the applicable parking fee.
9. EV charging fees are calculated separately.

## 6. Assumptions

- The facility contains multiple floors.
- Each floor has a fixed number of slots.
- Every vehicle has a valid registration number.
- Every started hour is charged as a complete hour.
- Two-wheelers are charged ₹10 per hour.
- Cars are charged ₹30 per hour.
- EV charging costs ₹12 per kWh.

## 7. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

Instead of using objects, the system can store vehicle information in parallel arrays.

Example:

- regNo[] stores registration numbers.
- type[] stores vehicle types.
- entryTime[] stores entry times.

| Comparison  | Objects | Parallel Arrays |
|---|---|---|
| Searching |   O(n) | O(n) |
| Data organization | Together | Separately |
| Adding attributes | Easy | Requires another array |
| Maintenance | Easy | Difficult |

Both approaches require O(n) space for n vehicles.

## 8. Conclusion

The object-oriented approach is selected because it organizes vehicle information properly, improves code reusability and makes the system easier to maintain and extend.
